# Prompt Lovable UX/UI
Build a fraud investigation app for healthcare claims called
"CAS Fraud Detector". This app helps a fraud investigator at a
health insurance company triage, investigate, and resolve fraud
alerts efficiently.

Connect to Supabase. The database has three tables:

1. "claims" — 50,000 healthcare claims with columns: CLM_ID (text, primary key),
   AT_PHYSN_NPI (text), specialty (text), HCPCS_CD (text), amount_billed (numeric),
   CLM_PMT_AMT (numeric), pm_score (numeric), structural_score (numeric),
   semantic_score (numeric), temporal_score (numeric), xgb_score (numeric),
   if_score (numeric), cas_score (numeric), oar_alert (integer 0 or 1),
   oar_confidence (numeric), n_signals (integer 0-3), is_fraud (integer),
   fraud_type (text), fraud_narrative (text), PRVDR_STATE_CD (text),
   service_hour (integer), submission_lag (integer), flow_length (integer)

2. "providers" — 800 providers with columns: AT_PHYSN_NPI (text, primary key),
   specialty (text), n_claims (integer), fraud_rate (numeric), cas_score (numeric),
   avg_pm_score (numeric), avg_amount (numeric), n_alerts (integer),
   PRVDR_STATE_CD (text)

3. "alerts" — fraud alerts with columns: id (uuid, primary key),
   CLM_ID (text), AT_PHYSN_NPI (text), status (text: pending/investigating/
   confirmed/dismissed), n_signals (integer), oar_confidence (numeric),
   fraud_narrative (text), created_at (timestamptz)

The app has 3 pages with a clean sidebar navigation:

PAGE 1 — "Alert Queue" (main page, the investigator's daily starting point)
This is a work queue, not a dashboard. The investigator opens this every
morning to see what needs attention.

Top section: 4 simple stat cards showing:
  - Pending alerts (count of alerts where status = 'pending')
  - Under investigation (count where status = 'investigating')
  - Confirmed fraud this month (count where status = 'confirmed')
  - Total amount at risk (sum of amount_billed from claims joined with
    alerts where status = 'pending')

Below the stats: a filterable, sortable table of alerts. Columns:
  Claim ID, Provider, Specialty, Amount, Signals (show as 1/3, 2/3, 3/3
  with colored dots — green for 1, amber for 2, red for 3), Confidence
  (percentage), Status (badge: pending=gray, investigating=blue,
  confirmed=red, dismissed=muted), and a "Review" button.

The table should:
  - Default sort by n_signals descending, then oar_confidence descending
    (highest priority first)
  - Have filter tabs at the top: All, Pending, Investigating, Confirmed,
    Dismissed
  - Show clear empty states if a filter has no results
  - Paginate at 20 rows per page

When the user clicks "Review" on any row, navigate to that claim's
detail page.

PAGE 2 — "Claim Detail" (the investigation workspace)
This is where the investigator actually works a case. Show:

Header: Claim ID, provider NPI, specialty, current status badge, and
the amount billed in large text.

Section "Why was this flagged?" — This is the most important section.
Show the fraud_narrative text in a prominent card with a light amber
background. Below it, show three horizontal progress bars labeled
"Process conformance", "Pattern analysis", and "Anomaly detection"
representing pm_score, xgb_score, and if_score (0 to 1 scale, colored
green under 0.3, amber 0.3-0.6, red above 0.6). Next to them show the
signal count as "X of 3 signals active".

Section "Claim details" — A clean two-column grid showing:
  Left: CPT code, Diagnosis code, Service hour, Submission lag (days),
  Process steps (flow_length)
  Right: Amount billed, Amount paid, Provider state, Provider specialty

Section "Provider context" — A small card showing the provider's
overall stats: total claims, alert rate, behavioral score
(cas_score as a colored badge: low if under 0.4, medium 0.4-0.7,
high above 0.7), and average billed amount.

Section "Actions" — Four clear buttons:
  - "Start investigation" (changes status to investigating, shows
    confirmation toast "Investigation started")
  - "Confirm as fraud" (changes status to confirmed, shows toast
    "Marked as confirmed fraud")
  - "Dismiss" (changes status to dismissed, shows toast "Alert dismissed")
  - "Back to queue" (returns to Alert Queue)

When status changes, update the alerts table in Supabase and show a
success toast. The button for the current status should be disabled
(e.g., if status is already "investigating", that button is grayed out).

PAGE 3 — "Provider Overview"
A searchable list of providers. Show a card for each with: NPI,
specialty, state, total claims, number of alerts, and a behavioral
score indicator (cas_score shown as Low/Medium/High with color).
Click a provider to see their alert history (list of their claims
that have oar_alert = 1, with status badges).

DESIGN:
- Use shadcn/ui components and Tailwind CSS
- Light theme (white background, not dark) — this is a professional
  work tool used 8 hours a day, readability matters
- Primary color: navy (#0C2340) for headers and sidebar
- Accent: teal (#0F6E56) for positive actions and confirmed states
- Warning: amber (#854F0B) for the "flagged" narrative section
- Alert: coral (#993C1D) for high-severity indicators
- Clean typography, generous spacing, no visual clutter
- Every action should have visible feedback (toasts, state changes)
- Sidebar should show the current page highlighted
- Mobile-responsive is nice but desktop-first is fine

On the Alert Queue page, add a small summary line above the table
that says "Showing X alerts" with the current filter count. Also
add a search input that filters the table by Claim ID or Provider NPI.

On the Claim Detail page, add a "Last updated" timestamp below the
status badge showing when the status was last changed. Also make the
"Why was this flagged?" section the first thing visible without
scrolling.

Make sure the sidebar shows a small counter badge next to "Alert Queue"
with the number of pending alerts.

Add a small "About" link in the sidebar footer (not as a main nav item).
It should open a simple page explaining:

"CAS Fraud Detector helps insurance fraud investigators prioritize
and resolve healthcare claim alerts. The system analyzes claims using
three independent methods — process conformance checking, pattern
recognition, and anomaly detection — and combines their signals to
rank alerts by confidence level.

Claims where all three methods agree are flagged with the highest
priority. Each alert includes a plain-language explanation of why
it was flagged, so investigators can quickly decide whether to
pursue or dismiss a case.

Built for SheBuilds Season 4."
